MiOS Hypr3D frame rate (applies after hypr3d-mios-edge-quality.patch). The room follows the desktop frame-rate
policy ([desktop.framerate], operator 2026-10-23: performance 90, balanced / power-saver / battery 60, gaming
120, unlocked = the output maximum; glass refraction and reflection at 1/3 of the desktop rate, 1/2 for gaming
and unlocked; the wallpaper at 1/2, the full rate when unlocked). miosd framerate and mios-gen
hyprland-3d-toggle (SUPER+G) send the running tier's rates; every key is partial and live, and 0 keeps the
upstream behaviour:

- render.max_fps (0..1000; 0 = the fixed 8 ms pump). The frame pump that keeps the room animated asks for a
  frame on phase-locked deadlines 1/max_fps apart (a late tick resyncs instead of bursting). The compositor cap
  (mios-pacing) stays the hard limit; this stops the room damaging the output for frames the cap defers anyway,
  and holds the room near D where no compositor cap runs.
- render.glass_fps (0..1000; 0 = every frame) and render.glass_cache_mib (default 256). The glass backdrop (the
  per-batch copy of the scene behind a pane, frosted with the preset's blur_sigma: the refraction) refreshes on
  due frames only. Each glass batch keeps its frosted copy in a slot texture (key: the window -- WindowRender::id,
  carried on every BSP piece; its snapshot texture is re-allocated by captures -- and the batch's ordinal within
  it) and frames in between bind that slot instead of copying and blurring again. A scene size, scale or MSAA change
  drops every slot, so the next frame refreshes; a due frame drops the slots no batch used. Batches beyond the
  budget refresh every frame. The dome the glass REFLECTS is latched into a copy on due frames as well, so
  reflection content follows glass_fps; reflection and refraction geometry stay per frame. Trade-off (what the
  policy asks for): while the camera turns, the screen-space refraction lags by up to (desktop / glass - 1)
  frames.
- world.live_fps (0..1000; 0 = every frame). The live wallpaper dome is re-captured (a full re-render of the
  background layer) only when due; between due frames the previous capture stays bound. The wallpaper itself
  steps at the same rate W, so no wallpaper step is lost.
- world.panorama_mirror and world.live_wrap become partial like every other key: a config call without them (miosd
  sends world = {live_fps = W} alone) no longer resets them to false / "mirror".
- The status file gains "pacing: max_fps= glass_fps= live_fps= glassRefresh/s= glassReuse/s= domeCapture/s=
  domeKept/s= glassSlots= glassMiB= envLatch= glassDue= glassKept= glassMiss=" (rates per second since the
  previous dump; the last three are totals of due frames, kept frames and kept-frame batches without a slot).
  The glass cache is freed when the room closes. Measured in a nested session (AMD iGPU, one animated window =
  22 glass batches, scene 1920x1080, no MSAA): glass_fps 0 (upstream) 16.5 compositor fps; glass_fps 30
  35 fps; glass_fps 5 73 fps -- every batch copy + frost is a full-scene pass.

